<h2>The family contest</h2>
<p>The testator executed a registered Will in October 2013 leaving a 21-bigha holding to his son. The grandson relies on a later document of February 2014 said to bequeath roughly half that holding to him. The testator died in November 2014; mutation followed on the registered Will within a month. The grandson\'s revenue proceedings failed, and in 2023 he applied for probate of the 2014 document. In January 2026 the Additional District Judge accepted the son\'s Order VII Rule 11 objection, held the 2014 document was not a Will at all, and rejected the probate application — without deciding limitation. The appeal challenged both the method and the outcome.</p>

<h2>What the High Court corrected</h2>
<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old">
    <h3>The trial court\'s approach</h3>
    <p>Read the 2014 document at the threshold, pronounced conclusively on its legal character, rejected the application, and left limitation — a potentially dispositive question — undecided.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">→</div>
  <div class="col new">
    <h3>The appellate correction</h3>
    <p>Prima facie testamentary recitals take the document past the gate; validity, Section 63 compliance and proof are trial questions; limitation under Article 137 must be decided first, independently and by reasoned order — then the merits.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>The two doctrinal strands</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>No mini-trial under Order VII Rule 11:</strong> the rule tests the proceeding on its own averments and annexures. A document whose recitals disclose a disposition, to an identified beneficiary, intended to operate on death, cannot be conclusively characterised against the applicant at the threshold.</li>
  <li><strong>Limitation needs reasons, not reflexes:</strong> the court rejected both extremes — the view that limitation is always a mixed question needing evidence, and the temptation to ignore it. When the right to apply accrued must be assessed against the record, including the applicant\'s earlier revenue proceedings and knowledge of the mutation.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>Lessons for succession practice</h2>
<p>Threshold applications are attractive because they promise an early exit, but they fail — and cost years — when aimed at questions that need evidence. A caveator who believes a rival document is a fabrication or fails Section 63 should welcome a prompt trial on proof, where attesting witnesses, handwriting evidence and suspicious circumstances can be deployed, rather than invite an appellate remand by seeking a conclusive finding at the gate. Conversely, propounders of later documents should treat limitation as a live threat: Article 137\'s three-year clock, running from accrual of the right to apply, is unforgiving of applicants who litigated around the estate for years before seeking probate, and the High Court has pointedly directed that inquiry here. The six-month disposal timeline the court suggested reflects a broader appellate impatience with succession disputes stalled at the pleadings stage.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> In probate matters, plead the accrual narrative affirmatively — when the applicant learned of the death, the rival instrument and the mutation, and what explains any gap. An application silent on these dates invites exactly the limitation battle this case now returns to fight.</p>
</div>
